 in a file  in your desk somewhere whether it's on a  computer whether it's in a data center  you have to have a plan for  the unfathomable for disasters for uh  having to recover your data for a  national disaster or what about like a  spy or national disaster just like  floods yes you have to have a plan for  as much as you possibly can and you i  don't think you can plan for everything  but data recovery is one of those things  and redundancy is one of those things so  you know that there's a what about  godzilla all right i'm about to quit  there's a lot that could happen and and  i'm sure everyone here can go into more  uh but one of the things that google  does really well  is they they duplicate data when  necessary they have multiple zones they  have regions so someone um like a  customer can say hey i want to make sure  that my data is  replicated across zones so that if a  zone goes down i can have uh data still  up and online and so that's one of the  things that you have to do when you're  securing your own  information is set up a disaster or data  recovery plan and google has some of  this in place too at the data center  level to help protect you uh but like  seth is and stephanie were saying  there's so many different cases like do  you guys have a little bit more about  how to actually set this up or  or how much of it is google's  responsibility versus how much is the  customer's responsibility yeah and  something that we already talked about  is that the sock the security operations  center of all google data centers are  also designed to roll up to a regional  sock for multi-regional high  availability purposes so you know if an  on-site sock were to be disrupted due to  an event such as a natural disaster or  an emergency building evacuation then  monitoring instantly rolls over to the  regional sock for continuous  uninterrupted monitoring so socks act as  a center for emergency and safety  response in addition to security  responsibilities like seth mentioned  earlier  you know these socks monitor and respond  to alarms at all of our facilities and  are constantly tracking local and global  events that could impact operations at  our data centers so really you can think  of this like high availability for  physical security and that means it's  high availability for logical security  and data protection as well  and then one thing that is cool to  mention is that we do run dozens of  drills a year paying an unannounced  skilled adversaries to try to get past  controls by any means necessary except  for violence and this includes  everything from fake badges to social  engineering fake ups trucks ladder  trucks to get over the fence people  actually hidden in trucks or attempts in  different environments like thick fog  and you know we usually win  after every attempt the main thing is  that we want to run a blameless  postmortem to improve and make the next  attempt even harder and we ensure the  strength of our security controls and  then we iterate controls as needed so  you know this is all about building a  culture of security  yeah and we also have an exercise at  google which we call dirt uh disaster  and recovery testing which affects both  the software security side and the data  center security side where to directly  answer this question like what happens  if there's a fire at a data center we  have in the past completely severed a  connection to a data center to make sure  that our disaster recovery protocols are  in place because yeah you can write  something down you can put it into  google doc you can print it out and put  it in a safe but an untested backup plan  is not a plan so we actually write and  we've we've written multiple white  papers about this where we actually do  some crazy stuff like we'll cut off the  entire mountain view office and all of  our senior executive cell phones and  make them inaccessible and make sure the  organization can continue to function  from a business continuity perspective  but we'll also take an entire data  center you know quote offline by maybe  severing the fiber connection  to make sure that our disaster recovery  plans are in place  all right so our final question of the  day comes from martin w who asks i'd  love to work in a data center so i have  to ask how does one go about getting a  job at a data center so we love this  question seth do you have any tips for  martin  yeah so you can always check out the  google careers page and i think we'll  either drop a link in the comments or uh  we'll show it on the screen here um but  we actually have a lot of openings in  our data centers we also have a lot of  openings for software engineering  positions site reliability engineering  positions um the only one that  guarantees you'll see the inside of a  data center is the data center  engineering positions but you should  always check out the google careers page  uh you can filter by location including  remote work  and we're always opening up you know new  new positions and new areas you can also  set up alerts to get an email anytime a  position opens up that fits your custom  filter criteria i so  sorry i just so really want to try and  get a job sneaking into the data centers  like if that's on that page i'm signing  up today  those are usually external firms i  actually used to work for one you won't  find that on my resume or linkedin but  um it's a really fun job but  uh you will occasionally end up in jail  um at least for a night while they get  ahold of the cso or the cio to confirm  your story that you were in fact hired  by the agency that you were  penetrating at the time yeah and i'm  also hosting the second season of where  the internet lives podcast and that's  where we explore more roles like  environment health and safety operations  mechanical engineers data center  technicians and hardware operations  managers so check that out we're going  to be diving into the background of the  people who are employed at data centers  and you'll probably learn about some  roles that you don't even know about yet  um we also are creating this animated  series on our youtube channel here it's  called discovering data centers where we  explore the innovations that exist at  our data centers and how we physically  build our data centers all the way down  to the software stack that makes our  technology built at the data center so  distributed and at scale lastly i do  want to just mention that the career  site is going to be included  in the links here so that you can check  it out and we are always hiring for  roles even internships all over the  world so right now we have open rules in  singapore japan all over the u.s and  chile so be sure to check that out  all right well that's all the time that  we have for today  unfortunately but thank you so much for  everyone joining our ask google cloud  live chat we hope that your questions  got answered we're going to be back with  more live chats throughout the year as a  reminder we'll be featuring your  questions from youtube instagram twitter  so send in your questions using the  hashtag askgooglecloud and comment below  if you have a question that we didn't  get to today so thanks everyone for  watching we'll see you all next time and  seth and carter stay out of trouble  [Music]  you

